Compiler result: No public overload accepts undefined as a second argument. Diagnostic 2345. This intentionally failing example is separate from the runnable solution.

after:
```typescript
function badge(text: string): string;
function badge(text: string, count: number): string;
function badge(text: string, count?: number): string {
  if (count === undefined) return text;
  return text + " (" + count + ")";
}
console.log(badge("Stars"));
console.log(badge("Stars", 4));
```
This prints `Stars`, then `Stars (4)`. Both calls use the same body. The first leaves out the count; the second supplies `4`.
